had sifted through them so I got  a hold of them and started looking  through them and a bunch of things just  immediately about including a $173  override in Minneapolis where bartoleme  used to live I thought you know I bet he  went to Prince's house on the Publix  nine and lo and behold that's exactly  what he did and during that trip he also  too spent money on a major league  baseball game the twins and from there  we started looking into other things  like trips to the Galapagos Islands  there's class plane trips to all over  the country and things like that  bartoleme who is the senior reporter on  the postern couriers investigative team  says they decided to expand their probe  into all official expense reports so we  thought what are other islands of  government in South Carolina that exist  on their own without much scrutiny and  sheriff's immediately came to mind and  as we basically we saw  every spending record we could get from  every county in the state all 46  counties suspended Greenville County  sheriff will Lewis is facing more  charges tonight  among those highlighted by Barlow me was  sheriff will Lewis of Greenville County  now facing criminal charges that he was  using public money to carry on a sexual  relationship with an employee who has  now claimed she was raped he has denied  the charges and her claims her  allegations that basically forced her to  have sex forced her to do other things  in and used that fear of termination  losing her job as a as a cudgel next  with the sheriff of Florence County  people who live in the area are speaking  out after the man they elected sheriff  Kenny Boone was arrested and charged  with embezzlement and misconduct in  office Sheriff Kenny Boone is also now  facing state charges as a result of bar  lemis reporting he too has pleaded not  guilty again it was all these these  these sort of questionable expenses for  personal gain the most serious charges  came following Bartleby's story on  sheriff alex Underwood Underwood had a  pattern of misbehavior ranging from  assaults he'd assaulted a couple of fire  chiefs at a traffic stop and also a man  was live-streaming traffic wreck as the  man was streaming his video on Facebook  sheriff Underwood is seen approaching  him and can be heard challenging him  there was a scuffle essentially  Underwood and his deputies threw the guy  in jail cooked up a bunch of bogus  charges and then tried to cover up when  they FBI began investigate sheriff  Underwood is now under federal  indictment he has pleaded not guilty to  the charges when we put it all together  we produced you know a very large  package of stories that really kinda  captured the scope and  significance of and history of why  sheriff's have gotten into trouble  Bartleby says his editors no such  investigative reporting takes time and  money but that they have decided it is  the only way local newspapers can  survive in the past few years we've  analyzed the data and we've found that  people are more likely to subscribe to  stories that are done with depth and  quality we found that the sheriff story  for instance generated the most  subscriptions of any story last year or  earlier this year on a local level it's  really the only way we'll be able to  survive and we are finding that  investigative journalism though it's  expensive is what keeps people reading  so our shout out tonight for Tony Marla  me and his editors demonstrating how  investigative reporting can do good and  do well on a personal note Tony's father  Joe was one of broadcast journalism is  great and I was fortunate enough to have  worked for him when I first started my  career at NBC News  nice to see Tony's carry on the  tradition of journalism with an impact  that's our program for tonight thanks  for joining us we'll see you back here  next week
 

